  "output": "Good — that confirms the full scope. This is a very early-stage Next.js console (only `sources` and `get-started` pages exist; `runs`/`conflicts`/`rules`/`audit` are nav links but not yet built). Given that, most of the requested categories genuinely don't apply yet — here's an honest breakdown rather than manufactured findings.\n\n## What actually exists to optimize\n\n**1. `countSourcesBySide` does a full table fetch + full-row Zod parse just to count rows** — `lib/server/sources.ts:23-30`\n\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();\n  return {\n    crm: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\").length,\n    warehouse: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"databricks\" || s.kind === \"snowflake\").length,\n    synthetic: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"synthetic\").length,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nThis selects every column (including the `jsonb` `config`), runs it through `sourceSchema.parse()` for every row, then does three full-array scans in JS — just to get three counts. Today `sources` is small, but this is the shape of a real N+1/over-fetch pattern once it isn't. Push the aggregation into SQL:\n\n```ts\nimport { sql } from \"drizzle-orm\";\n\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  await connection();\n  const [row] = await getDb()\n    .select({\n      crm: sql<number>`count(*) filter (where ${sources.kind} in ('salesforce','hubspot'))`,\n      warehouse: sql<number>`count(*) filter (where ${sources.kind} in ('databricks','snowflake'))`,\n      synthetic: sql<number>`count(*) filter (where ${sources.kind} = 'synthetic')`,\n    })\n    .from(sources);\n  return row;\n}\n```\n\n**2. No caching on `listSources()`** — every call to it re-queries Postgres, and it's called independently from two routes (`/sources`, and indirectly from `/get-started` via `countSourcesBySide`). Not a bug today since they're separate requests, but once `listSources()` is called more than once per request (e.g. a layout + a page both needing it), wrap it in React's `cache()`:\n\n```ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\nexport const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => { ... });\n```\n\nThis dedupes calls within a single render pass for free — worth adding preemptively since it's a one-line change and this data-access pattern will get reused as `/runs`, `/conflicts`, etc. are built.\n\n## What I checked and found clean/not-applicable\n\n- **N+1 queries**: None. Only single-table `select().from(sources)` queries exist; no relational fetches with per-row lookups yet (the `matches`/`conflicts`/`entities` tables aren't queried by any page yet).\n- **Unnecessary React re-renders**: No `useState`, `useEffect`, `useMemo`, or `useCallback` anywhere in the codebase. The app is nearly all Server Components; the handful of `\"use client\"` files (`nav-rail.tsx`, `sign-out-control.tsx`, button, etc.) are small leaf components reading `usePathname`/`useUser` with no derived-state or prop-drilling issues.\n- **Memory leaks**: None — no subscriptions, intervals, or event listeners to clean up.\n- **Redundant computations**: Only the count-via-filter pattern above; nothing else duplicated.\n- **DB connection handling** (`lib/db/index.ts`): Already done right — connection is lazily created and cached on `globalThis` to survive HMR, with `prepare: false` correctly set for Supabase's transaction pooler.\n\n## One forward-looking note\n\nThe comment in `lib/server/sources.ts` says \"TanStack Query is reserved for run-status polling only\" — when `/runs` gets built with live status polling, watch for the classic polling leak (interval not cleared on unmount) and prefer TanStack Query's built-in `refetchInterval` (auto-cleans on unmount) over a raw `setInterval` in a `useEffect`.\n\nThere isn't more to find right now — the codebase is too early-stage for N+1s or render-thrashing to exist yet. Want me to apply the `countSourcesBySide` SQL fix and the `cache()` wrap now?\n",
